Depression in terminally ill
Palliative & End of Life
Pervasive sad mood, Complete anhedonia, Persistent hopelessness/despair

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-021
Reason for Visit: Acute psychiatric crisis featuring complete refusal of essential medications and overtly expressed desire for hastened death.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute escalation of profound despair, with the patient barricading themselves in bed, aggressively refusing life-sustaining symptom-control medications, and explicitly voicing suicidal ideation. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to conduct an acute risk assessment, secure the physical environment, and arrange emergency psychiatric support prior to morning, as the patient poses an immediate risk of self-harm and withdrawal from necessary medical care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Leave me alone. I am nothing but a burden, and I just want to die tonight before I ruin your life any further."
History of Present Illness: Over the past three weeks, the patient has exhibited a profound, unremitting depressed mood and severe pervasive sadness. The adult child reports complete anhedonia characterized by an absolute loss of interest in all previously enjoyed activities. The patient verbalizes severe feelings of worthlessness, deep guilt, and explicitly feeling like an unmanageable burden to loved ones. Tonight, there is explicit, profound hopelessness and complete despair regarding the future and the end-of-life process. The patient has active, expressly communicated suicidal ideation and a profound, persistent desire for hastened death. There is severe social withdrawal and active, persistent isolation from close family and friends. During the visit, the patient demonstrates a distinctly altered, profoundly flat emotional affect interspersed with highly tearful spells. There is out of proportion cognitive slowing and decreased ability to concentrate or make basic decisions. Somatic symptoms frequently overlapping with the underlying terminal disease include severe anorexia and intractable insomnia, completely failing to sleep for the last 48 hours.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but strictly bedbound by choice due to severe apathy.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate, critical risk of intentional medication overdose or self-harm; profound emotional distress of the adult child acting as caregiver.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient is lying in the fetal position, avoiding eye contact, speaking in a flat, monotone whisper.
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals no acute signs of physical deterioration or focal neurological deficits. Cognitive assessment reveals delayed processing speed and severe psychomotor retardation, without the profound inattention characteristic of delirium. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed pervasive sad mood (tearfulness, flat affect), complete anhedonia (absolute refusal of family interaction), and persistent hopelessness/despair (verbalizing desire for immediate death).
Environmental Observations: A large box of unsecured prescription opioid analgesics and sedatives is present on the bedside table, presenting a highly lethal and accessible means of overdose.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has missed tonight's scheduled analgesic and anticonvulsant medications due to deliberate refusal. Not currently on any scheduled antidepressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Depression in terminally ill - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Suicidal Crisis)
Differentials:

Hypoactive delirium - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient remains fully oriented, able to form complex logical (though depressive) thoughts, and lacks the acute, severe inattention required for a delirium diagnosis.

Appropriate preparatory grief - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of active suicidal ideation, profound feelings of worthlessness, and complete cessation of all life-sustaining activities, which extend far beyond normal emotional processing.

Uncontrolled somatic pain - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient explicitly denies severe physical pain, citing pure psychic agony as the driver for the desire to hasten death.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the midst of a severe, acute major depressive crisis, completely distinct from expected end-of-life grief. The presence of active, verbalized suicidal ideation, profound psychomotor retardation, and the deliberate refusal of essential palliative medications constitutes an immediate psychiatric emergency. Leaving the patient unassessed until standard hours would subject them to extreme psychological suffering and leave an unsecured, lethal means of overdose in the room with a highly vulnerable individual and a distressed caregiver. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to secure the environment, administer an acute anxiolytic/sedative to break the immediate panic, and facilitate emergency psychiatric triage.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a formal suicide risk assessment. Instructed the adult child to immediately confiscate and lock away all medications in the home. Administered a stat dose of sublingual lorazepam to relieve the acute psychic tension and assist with sleep. Negotiated a strict safety contract for the next 12 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the overwhelming urge to completely isolate yourself (trigger), you must explicitly agree to sit in the living room with your child for just ten minutes (behavior). This tiny break in isolation forces a minor interruption to the depressive thought loop, keeping you physically visible and safe in that critical moment (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency referral to the community palliative care social worker and clinical psychologist for an urgent morning review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Immediate removal of all sharp objects, belts, and medications from the patient's bedroom.
Follow-up: Multidisciplinary team review tomorrow morning; GP to initiate rapid-acting psychostimulants or appropriate antidepressants based on prognosis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient makes an active attempt at self-harm, attempts to abscond from the home, or explicitly breaks the safety contract and refuses to stay visible to the caregiver, the adult child must immediately call 000 for emergency police and ambulance attendance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe depression is a secondary chemical complication of the illness, not a personal failing or a required part of the dying process.
Clinical Course: With the introduction of psychiatric support and appropriate medication, the profound despair can be medically lifted, improving the quality of the remaining time.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Caregiver: Do not leave the patient alone under any circumstances tonight. You must physically administer all future medications and retain strict control of the supply.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Patient) The illness has caused a severe chemical imbalance in your brain, plunging you into an artificial darkness that hides your true value to your family. The medication tonight will force your brain to rest and sleep. Tomorrow, we will bring in expert help to lift this chemical weight.
Actionable Steps: Agree to the safety contract for tonight. Allow your child to manage all medications and stay in the room with you.

